{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And Aaron spoke to Moses.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory (Torat Kohanim) said that וַיְדַבֵּר ('spoke') is nothing but a term of harshness, indicating difficult words. One must know how this matter can be justified in Aaron's conduct toward Moses. And perhaps it is because Moses' words to his surviving sons were a kind of speech that resembled a curse, and he was still in trembling of heart over his two sons who had already died that very day, and the adversary was prosecuting. And all the more so when he heard the pressing of Moses' lips in the semblance of a curse — therefore his heart trembled and he spoke in a loud voice before Moses, for his compassion was stirred toward his sons." "<b>Behen, today.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Zevaḥim 101a) that his intention is to say: Was it then on this day that Eleazar and Ithamar offered, who are forbidden to offer while in the mourning of אֲנִינוּת — yet you say perhaps they offered it in אֲנִינוּת and therefore you burned it? It is I who offered it, and a High Priest may offer while an אוֹנֵן." "And the plain sense of the verse is along this line: <b>Behold, today they offered</b> — meaning, Nadab and Abihu their sin-offering and their burnt-offering before HaShem, etc., his intention being that he is an אוֹנֵן, for on that very day they died. <b>And I ate the sin-offering of the day</b> — meaning, the sin-offering of the day, which is the goat of the new moon, and here he intimated to him that this differs from the meal-offering, which is קָדְשֵׁי שָׁעָה (sanctities of the occasion), but the sin-offering of the day is קָדְשֵׁי דוֹרוֹת (sanctities for all generations) — would it have been pleasing in the eyes of HaShem to eat it?" "And it would appear possible to prove from here one question: whether it is permitted for a disciple to rule for himself a ruling in the place of his master, for I saw the Rosh, may his memory be for blessing (Eruvin 63a), who wrote to prohibit this unless it is שַׁר סַכִּינָא (an imminent life-threatening situation), where the concern is not so severe. Now, from the fact that we see that Aaron ruled this halakhah for himself and they burned the sin-offering, it is implied that it is permitted. And you have no greater master for Aaron than Moses. Or it may be that one can reject this, for this ruling is not [truly] a ruling, as they said in Yoma (Rashi there, Yoma 5a) — that Aaron reasoned a fortiori: concerning the lighter matter, the Torah said ״לֹא אָכַלְתִּי בְאוֹנִי״ ('I did not eat in my mourning'); the more sacred matter — how much the more so! — end. And since the law of the tithe was stated to him from the mouth of Moses, it is as though Moses had told him to burn [it], and he did not rule for himself at all; for the sin-offering is included within the law of the tithe by a fortiori inference, and such a case is permitted for a disciple to rule on, and it is lighter than בֵּיעֲתָא בְּכוּתְחָא (a gourd with cold water). And see the words of the Tosafot there (Eruvin 62b) for the meaning of הוֹרָאַת בֵּיעֲתָא בְּכוּתְחָא."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {3 "The phrase בֵּיעֲתָא בְּכוּתְחָא is Aramaic for 'a roasted gourd with cold water' — a Talmudic idiom for an obvious, simple ruling; transliterated here as in the original."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:55:07.167781312Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}